117-410">c</xref></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NA</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NA</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn13-ehp-117-410"><label>a</label><p>Ninety-three percent (42 of 45) of these tube wells are unsafe; that is, only 7% (3 of 45) of these tube wells do not exceed any of these WHO health-based drinking water guidelines.</p></fn><fn id="tfn14-ehp-117-410"><label>b</label><p>Not applicable; the WHO has not established a health-based drinking water guideline for Fe or Zn (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b34-ehp-117-410">WHO 1996a</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b36-ehp-117-410">1998a</xref>).</p></fn><fn id="tfn15-ehp-117-410"><label>c</label><p>The severity of chronic As poisoning in Bangladesh might be magnified by a lack of Se or Zn or both (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b15-ehp-117-410">Frisbie et al. 2002</xref>; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b26-ehp-117-410">Ortega et al. 2003</xref>).</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>